    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A republic of 1819–1831 joining Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-granada">New Granada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quito">Quito</a> and Panama, proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#congress-of-angostura">Congress of Angostura</a>.</li><li>Its armies, led by Bolívar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sucre">Sucre</a>, ended Spanish rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-america">South America</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#junin-region">Junín</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> in 1824.</li><li>Regional rivalries, debt and disputes over Bolívar’s power broke it into Colombia, Venezuela and Ecuador by 1831.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Its official name was simply the Republic of Colombia; historians later coined ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#esztergom">Gran</a> Colombia’ to distinguish it from the present-day country.</p></aside>
    </section>

